Regular Expression /RegEx
-group of character to find the match pattern
-to use RegEx we use re module
-it provides symbol,character etc...
-so import re module

eg:
s1="Python is very much advanced and it has 3.13,3.12,3.14 version"
*to find python in s1 or not we can do
-print("Python" in s1)

*to find its index
-print(s1.find("Python")
-it gives the first index from where Python start in here it will give index of P

*what if we have to search the pattern  of version so we use RegEx

#1:re.search(regex_pattern,string)
-when there is match found it return (match object)
-it gives where to where(index/span) the Character is found
-last index is excluded

*meta character-symbol,special character

#search using pattern
s1="Python is very much advanced and it has 3.13,3.12,3.14 version"
src=re.search("[0-9][0-9]",s1)
print(src)

*here it search first two consecutive number from 0-9 and 0-9
 so the first consecutive number is 13 so it gives 13 from 3.13
 where 13 is consecutive number

*what if to find 3.13 or any number with . we use second meta character(.)

#2: .  -->meta character
 -it matches . and any character except newline

eg1:
s1="Python is very much advanced and it has 3.13,3.12,3.14 version"
src=re.search("[0-9].[0-9][0-9]",s1)
print(src)
-it will give first number where this pattern occur =3.13

eg2:
src2=re.search("[0-9].[0-9][0-9]","House number 123/A")
print(src2)
-it will give 123 but the pattern is different so . matches any character except newline here 1 . 3 in place of . =2

#[.]
-it will look for exact . position in a pattern

eg:
src2=re.search("[0-9][.][0-9][0-9]","House number 123/A")
print(src2)
-it will give none cause there is not exact . position after 1

#3.Special character in regex
#r"old\new"
-r is a special character where strings are kept raw eg if we dont use r it will give result old ew but due to r it will give old\new

#\d
-\d represent 1 digit character similar to [0-9]

#\D
-\D opposite of \d non-digit character

#\s
-\s it matches any whitespace character tab and newline character

#\S
-\S is opposite of \s it will it matches any character except space,\n,\t

#\w
-it matches [a-z],[A-Z],[0-9],_

#\W
-it is opposite of \w it matches any character except [a-z],[A-Z],[0-9],_

4.Quantifiers
-Symbol or character used for how many quantity we want to match

# +:-matches one or more repetition of particular character
Syntax: r"[A-Z][a-z]+"

# *:-zero or more
Syntax:r"[A-Z][a-z]*"

# ?:-Zero or one repetition of the previous pattern
Syntax:r"[A-Z][a-z]?"

# {n}:-n number or character
syntax:r"[a-z]{4}" =it will run upto 4 character
       r"[A-Z][a-z]{4}" =here the 4 will work for lower latter not upper
       r"[A-Z][a-z]{2,4}" =in curly bracket it means that only it will match lower cast between 1 to 4
